{"translation": "In those cases where the upper [fragments], slipping, have passed to the side or lower down, it would contribute to the correction if the person were to lie supine, and something a little higher were placed beneath him in the region between the shoulder blades, so that the chest is as far as possible bent over [περιρρηδές]; and [it would contribute] if someone were to draw the upper arm upward, stretched out alongside the ribs. And if the physician, with one hand, placing the flat of the hand on the head of the upper arm, were to push it away, and with the other were to set the broken bones in line, in this way he would best bring them into their natural position. But, as has already been said, the upper bone is very much inclined to slip in under, downward. In most cases, then, once they have been bandaged, the posture helps: with the elbow held right against the ribs, the shoulder is in this way forced upward. And there are some in whom one must force the shoulder upward, as has been said. So when the part of the collarbone that is continuous with the sternum slips either to the side or downward, he says that it then helps to prepare for the correction if, having laid the person down supine, one places beneath him between the shoulder blades, at the spine itself, a pillow, obviously, or a neck-cushion or something of the sort, so that the whole thorax becomes bent around at this point. For 'that it be bent over' (περιρρηδὲς ἔῃ) is said in this sense also in the Poet: 'and doubled over the table he fell.'", "renderings": [{"target_no": 21, "surface": "ὕπτιος", "english": "supine", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 37, "surface": "στῆθος", "english": "chest", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 42, "surface": "βραχίονα,", "english": "upper arm", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 141, "surface": "κλειδὸς", "english": "collarbone", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 166, "surface": "ὠμοπλατῶν", "english": "shoulder blades", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 1